.TH REPL 1 "@PROJECT_VERSION@" "@PROJECT_AUTHOR@" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
repl \- batch replace text in files (human editor)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B repl
[\fIOPTION\fR]... \fIPATTERN\fR \fIREPLACEMENT\fR [\fIFILE\fR]...
.br
.B repl
[\fIOPTION\fR]... \fB\-e\fR \fIPATTERN\fR ... \fIREPLACEMENT\fR [\fIFILE\fR]...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B repl
searches for
.I PATTERN
in each named
.I FILE
and replaces matches with
.IR REPLACEMENT .
Unlike a stream editor,
.B repl
edits files in place.
A file is rewritten only when the result differs from the original contents
(atomic write via a temporary file beside the target).
.PP
With no
.IR FILE
arguments,
.B repl
reads standard input and writes the substituted result to standard output.
.PP
When
.B \-e
and/or
.B \-f
supply patterns, the first remaining non-option argument is
.I REPLACEMENT
and any further arguments are files.
Otherwise the first two non-option arguments are
.I PATTERN
and
.IR REPLACEMENT .
.PP
Pattern matching follows
.BR grep (1)
conventions.
The default pattern type is fixed strings (\fB\-F\fR).
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Pattern selection
.TP
.BR \-F ", " \-\-fixed\-strings
Interpret patterns as fixed strings.
This is the default.
.TP
.BR \-E ", " \-\-extended\-regexp
Interpret patterns as POSIX extended regular expressions (ERE).
.TP
.BR \-G ", " \-\-basic\-regexp
Interpret patterns as POSIX basic regular expressions (BRE).
.TP
.BR \-P ", " \-\-perl\-regexp
Interpret patterns as Perl-compatible regular expressions using PCRE2.
.TP
.BR \-e " \fIPATTERNS\fR, " \-\-regexp=\fIPATTERNS\fR
Use
.I PATTERNS
as the pattern(s).
May be given multiple times and combined with
.BR \-f .
Useful to protect a pattern that begins with
.BR \- .
.TP
.BR \-f " \fIFILE\fR, " \-\-file=\fIFILE\fR
Obtain patterns from
.IR FILE ,
one per line.
An empty file contributes zero patterns (nothing matches).
Use
.B \-
to read patterns from standard input.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-ignore\-case
Ignore case distinctions in patterns and input data.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-ignore\-case
Do not ignore case (the default).
Useful in scripts to cancel a prior
.BR \-i .
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-invert\-match
Invert the sense of matching:
each line that does
.B not
match
.I PATTERN
is replaced entirely by
.IR REPLACEMENT .
Matching lines are left unchanged.
.TP
.BR \-w ", " \-\-word\-regexp
Select only matches that form whole words
(bounded by non-word characters or the ends of the line).
Word characters are letters, digits, and underscore.
Has no effect if
.B \-x
is also given.
.TP
.BR \-x ", " \-\-line\-regexp
Select only matches that span an entire line
(as if the pattern were anchored with
.B ^
and
.BR $ ).
.SS File selection
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-recursive
Read all files under each directory, recursively.
Follow symbolic links only when they appear on the command line.
.TP
.BR \-R ", " \-\-dereference\-recursive
Like
.BR \-r ,
but follow symbolic links wherever they are found.
.TP
.B \-\-max\-depth=\fINUM\fR
Limit how deep directory recursion may go.
Depth 0 means only the starting directory entries.
.TP
.B \-\-exclude=\fIGLOB\fR
Skip files whose base name matches
.IR GLOB
(shell-style, as in
.BR fnmatch (3)).
.TP
.B \-\-exclude\-from=\fIFILE\fR
Read exclude globs from
.IR FILE ,
one per line.
.TP
.B \-\-exclude\-dir=\fIGLOB\fR
Skip directories whose base name matches
.IR GLOB .
.TP
.B \-\-include=\fIGLOB\fR
Process only files whose base name matches
.IR GLOB .
If any
.B \-\-include
option is given, a file must match at least one include glob.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-directories=\fIACTION\fR
How to handle directories named on the command line:
.RS
.TP
.B repl
Treat the directory as an ordinary file (default; usually fails).
.TP
.B skip
Skip directories silently.
.TP
.B recurse
Same as
.BR \-r :
process files under each directory recursively.
.RE
.TP
.BR \-D ", " \-\-devices=\fIACTION\fR
How to handle device nodes, FIFOs, and sockets:
.B repl
(default) or
.BR skip .
.TP
.B \-\-binary\-files=\fITYPE\fR
Control binary-file handling:
.RS
.TP
.B binary
Default.
Perform replacement only in the leading text portion of the file,
stopping before the first non-printable byte
(tab, newline, carriage return, and form feed are allowed).
.TP
.B without\-match
Omit binary files entirely (do not modify them).
.TP
.B text
Treat the file as text; process the whole file.
.RE
.TP
.B \-I
Same as
.BR \-\-binary\-files=without\-match .
.TP
.BR \-U ", " \-\-binary
Treat files as binary and disable the printable-prefix heuristic
(process the whole buffer).
.TP
.BR \-z ", " \-\-null\-data
Treat input and output as sequences of lines terminated by a NUL byte
instead of a newline.
Affects line-oriented options such as
.B \-v
and
.BR \-x .
.SS Reporting
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet ", " \-\-silent
Do not print the default one-line summary of how many files changed.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-context
For each changed file, print a context diff as from
.BR "diff \-c" .
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-unified
For each changed file, print a unified diff as from
.BR "diff \-u" .
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-summary
Print a detailed batch summary: files examined, files changed,
replacement count, and binary files skipped.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a usage message and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print version information and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success (including when no file needed changing).
.TP
.B 1
A usage error occurred, a pattern failed to compile, or a file could not be processed.
.SH EXAMPLES
Replace a fixed string in one file:
.PP
.nf
repl foo bar notes.txt
.fi
.PP
Case-insensitive rename across a tree:
.PP
.nf
repl \-i \-r OldName NewName src/
.fi
.PP
Extended regexp digit wipe:
.PP
.nf
repl \-E \[aq][0\-9]+\[aq] \[aq]#\[aq] data.txt
.fi
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B LANGUAGE
.TQ
.B LC_ALL
.TQ
.B LC_MESSAGES
.TQ
.B LANG
Select the language for messages and translated man pages when available.
